% A name resolves to what the dictionary stack says it resolves to, now.
%
% What a name resolves to is remembered against a generation, so that a
% page setting the same names over and over does not walk the dictionary
% stack for each of them. The remembering is only sound while nothing has
% changed what the walk would answer, and this drives the ways it can.
%
% Each case defines a name, reads it, changes what it should resolve to,
% and reads it again. A reading that came from a remembered answer rather
% than from the stack shows here as the OLD value, and nowhere else: no
% error is raised and the page carries no mark, so only a comparison can
% see it.

% The count is kept in userdict by name rather than defined, because
% these cases run with dictionaries of their own pushed: a def would
% land the count in whichever one is current and the tally would be
% read back from an empty userdict as zero.
userdict /failures 0 put
/check { exch { pop }{ (FAIL: ) print print (\n) print
                       userdict /failures userdict /failures get 1 add put }
         ifelse } bind def

% --- the value under a name changes in the dictionary it was found in ---
/A 1 def
A 1 eq (a name reads what was defined) check
/A 2 def
A 2 eq (and reads the new value when it is defined again) check

% --- a dictionary is pushed that redefines it -------------------------
/D1 3 dict def
D1 /A 3 put
D1 begin
A 3 eq (a dictionary pushed over it answers instead) check
end
A 2 eq (and the one beneath answers once it is popped) check

% --- a dictionary is written to WHILE it is on the stack --------------
/D2 3 dict def
D2 begin
/B 10 def
B 10 eq (a name defined in the current dictionary reads) check
D2 /B 11 put
B 11 eq (and reads what a put into that dictionary left) check
end

% --- a dictionary is written to while it is NOT on the stack, and then
%     pushed. Nothing the walk answered before it was pushed can stand.
/D3 3 dict def
D3 /C 20 put
/C 99 def
C 99 eq (a name reads the definition on the stack) check
D3 begin
C 20 eq (and reads the pushed dictionary's, which was written off it) check
D3 /C 21 put
C 21 eq (and reads a further put into it while it is on the stack) check
end
C 99 eq (and the outer one again once it is popped) check

% --- undef removes a binding ------------------------------------------
/D4 3 dict def
D4 /E 30 put
D4 begin
E 30 eq (a name reads the pushed dictionary's binding) check
D4 /E undef
end
/E 31 def
E 31 eq (and reads elsewhere once that binding is gone) check

% --- store writes where the name is already defined --------------------
/F 40 def
/D5 3 dict def
D5 begin
/F 41 store
F 41 eq (store writes where the name was found) check
end
F 41 eq (and that is the outer definition it found) check

userdict /failures get 0 eq
    { (SUCCESS\n) print }
    { userdict /failures get =print ( failures\n) print } ifelse
flush
quit
